VERDICT -- brkoff
=================
TURNING OFF THE OPERATOR THAT WASTES A QUARTER OF THE BUDGET CHANGES ALMOST NOTHING.

        A=0.50      B=0.05    F=0.05+BRK=0   F vs B  | F paired vs B
  P1     510,450     571,668       571,668    +0.0%  |  -8.3  +6.3  +0.0
  P3   4,583,170   4,468,072     4,468,072    +0.0%  |  +0.1  +0.0  -0.6
  P27    859,349     692,860       692,860    +0.0%  |  +0.0  +0.0  +0.0
  P16  3,252,294   3,022,891     2,967,764    -1.8%  |  -1.8  -3.5  +2.6
  P7     860,744     749,573       750,119    +0.1%  |  +0.0  +0.3  -3.4
  P33    964,466     933,974       952,955    +2.0%  |  +0.0  +2.0  +2.0
  median-sum  A 11,030,473   B -5.36%   F -5.68% (vs B -0.34%)
  F beats B in 5 of 18 cells; ratio-mean -0.24%, geo-mean -0.28%.

THREE MEDIANS ARE IDENTICAL TO THE DIGIT -- P1, P3 and P27 return exactly B's value with brk off.
On P1 that is 571,668 with the same components, reached whether the search is handed 13.3 extra
seconds or not.  brk really does burn 24% of prob_1's budget for zero gain (opstat.log), and the
budget is genuinely recoverable; there is simply nothing to buy with it.

G CONFIRMS IT FROM THE OTHER SIDE.  prob_1 at the OLD reserve with brk off returns 676,002 and
508,193 -- both values A itself produces with brk ON, because at that reserve brk only takes 1.5 s.
The operator is irrelevant where it is cheap and useless where it is expensive.

WHAT THIS CLOSES.  Four routes have now been tried for prob_1's 29 unused seconds: a longer single
round (B, +12% by median), two rounds (D, +30%), three rounds (E, +42%), and freeing brk's slice
(F, 0.0%).  fillmoved.log had already ruled out the fill round.  The conclusion is not that the
knob was set wrong but that this pipeline converges on prob_1 well before its clock does.

SHIPPING DECISION UNCHANGED: B.  F's -0.34% rests on 5 wins in 18 cells, which is inside the noise
this study has measured repeatedly, and brk was ADOPTED on evidence (tasks 8 and 10) -- switching
it off band-wide on a wash would be trading a measured feature for nothing.
